INSTANCEMETHODS::

subsection:: Synesthesia sculpt words

The following methods are added by the Synesthesia quark
(see link::Guides/Synesthesia::). Effects and wiring occupy the proxy's source
slots from index 10 upward, so your own use of slots 0–9 is untouched. Numeric
parameters are lifted into named controls (with registered link::Classes/Spec::s),
so everything remains reachable via code::.set::, GUIs and presets.

METHOD:: fx
Insert (or retune) a named effect on this proxy, in place, after its source and any
previously added effects. Recipes currently available: code::\lpf:: and
code::\hpf:: (resonant filters; the value is the cutoff in Hz), code::\verb:: and
code::\delay:: (the value is the wet mix 0..1), code::\dist:: (drive 0..1),
code::\crush:: (bitcrush amount 0..1, requires sc3-plugins), code::\wide:: (stereo
width 0..2) and code::\dc:: (DC-blocker). The registry lives in
link::Classes/Synesthesia#*fxRecipes:: and is designed to be extended.

The value may be a number (lifted to a named control such as code::\lpf_freq::), or
any live signal — a control proxy, another Synesthesia word.
argument:: name
the effect's name in the recipe registry.
argument:: val
the effect's main parameter (number, proxy, or function). Defaults per recipe.
returns:: this proxy (chainable)
discussion::
code::
~a.fx(\lpf, 1200).fx(\verb, 0.3);   // insert two effects, chained call
~a.fx(\lpf, 500);                    // same name retunes in place (crossfaded)
~a.set(\lpf_freq, 2000);             // the lifted control is live
~a.fx(\lpf, pad(\x).mapExp(100, 8000));  // or drive it with a signal
::

METHOD:: unfx
Remove a named effect added with link::#-fx::.
argument:: name
the effect's name.
returns:: this proxy

METHOD:: wide
Stereo width via mid/side processing: 0 is mono, 1 unchanged, up to 2 exaggerated.
Sugar for code::this.fx(\wide, width)::.
argument:: width
0..2 (default 1)
returns:: this proxy

METHOD:: dc
Insert a DC blocker (link::Classes/LeakDC::) — useful hygiene for feedback patches
and chaotic sources. Sugar for code::this.fx(\dc, 1)::.
returns:: this proxy

METHOD:: fade
Set this proxy's crossfade time, returning the proxy so it chains with
code::.play::.
argument:: seconds
the new code::fadeTime:: (default 4)
returns:: this proxy
discussion::
code::
~a = \tone.stack(~r, [1, 2, 4]);
~a.fade(12).play;    // long, painterly transitions from here on
::

subsection:: Synesthesia wire words

METHOD:: feeds
Mix this proxy into another proxy's canvas: the signal enters strong::target:: at a
low slot, so it passes through everything strong::target::'s link::#-fx:: chain
does. The amount is lifted to a named control (code::<thisName>_feed::). Use it
for layering sources into one sculptable object, or for deliberate feedback
wiring.
argument:: target
the receiving link::Classes/NodeProxy::.
argument:: amt
mix amount (default 0.2).
returns:: this proxy
discussion::
code::
~t = \tone.drone(~r, 1.5); ~t.play;
~t.feeds(~a, 0.15);     // ~t is now also inside ~a — ~a.fx sculpts the sum
::

METHOD:: route
Send this proxy to a standing effect return created in your setup: by convention a
return named code::\verb:: is a pair of proxies code::~verbIn:: (a silent input
mix) and code::~verb:: (the effect reading it). code::route:: adds this proxy,
scaled, into code::~<name>In::. The amount is lifted to a named control unique to
this source and return.

note:: This word is called code::route:: rather than send because
link::Classes/NodeProxy:: already has a code::send:: method (it re-sends synths).
::
argument:: returnName
the return's base name (default code::\verb::, expecting code::~verbIn::).
argument:: amt
send amount (default 0.3).
returns:: this proxy
discussion::
code::
~k.route(\verb, 0.2);     // a little reverb on the kick
~m.route(\delay, 0.5);    // more delay on the melody
::

METHOD:: duck
Sidechain: dip this proxy's level by another proxy's amplitude envelope — the
classic "the kick carves the drone" move, and a bridge from the pattern world into
the signal world. The amount is lifted to the named control code::\duck_amt::.
argument:: source
the proxy whose loudness does the ducking.
argument:: amt
depth, 0..2 — at 1 a loud source fully silences this proxy (default 0.5).
argument:: attack
envelope follower attack in seconds (default 0.01).
argument:: release
envelope follower release in seconds (default 0.25).
returns:: this proxy
discussion::
code::
~k = \k.hits("x...x...");
~k.play;
~a.duck(~k, 0.6);         // the drone breathes with the kick
~a.set(\duck_amt, 1.2);   // deepen it live
::

subsection:: Synesthesia bridge words

METHOD:: follow
This proxy's amplitude envelope as a new control-rate proxy (named
code::<thisName>_env:: in the space) — the general pattern→signal bridge. Use the
result anywhere a signal fits: modulation, other proxies, link::#-fx:: values.
argument:: lagTime
smoothing, lifted to the named control code::\lag:: (default 0.1).
returns:: a control-rate link::Classes/NodeProxy::
discussion::
code::
~env = ~k.follow;
~shimmer = { SinOsc.ar([2000, 2003]) * ~env.kr(1) * 0.1 }; ~shimmer.play;
::

METHOD:: pat
This proxy's current control value as pattern material — the signal→pattern
bridge. Sugar for BenoitLib's code::Pkr(this):: (BenoitLib must be installed).
returns:: a pattern that repeatedly yields the proxy's latest value
discussion::
code::
~m = \ping.deg("0 3 5 7", 0.25, amp: pad(\y).pat.linlin(0, 1, 0.05, 0.5));
::

METHOD:: mapExp
A new control proxy mapping this proxy's 0..1 output exponentially into
strong::lo::..strong::hi:: — the right curve for frequencies and times.
argument:: lo
argument:: hi
returns:: a new control-rate link::Classes/NodeProxy::
discussion::
code::
~cut = pad(\x).mapExp(200, 8000);   // perceptually even filter sweep
::

METHOD:: mapLin
As link::#-mapExp:: but linear — for amounts, mixes and pans.
argument:: lo
argument:: hi
returns:: a new control-rate link::Classes/NodeProxy::

subsection:: Synesthesia breakbeat words

A break voice is a persistent voice you sculpt: one drum loop, locked to the session
tempo, sliceable from intact to dust. The tutorial is link::Guides/Breaks::; the
registry underneath is link::Classes/Break::. Every word below leaves the voice
running — none of them rebuild the synth.

METHOD:: brk
Put a break in this proxy. The first call installs the code::\break:: engine and
allocates this voice's slice-sequence buffer. Later calls swap the sample on the
next bar with no gap, keeping every other control exactly where you left it — the
grain generator reads its buffer once per grain, so grains already sounding finish
on the old break while new ones read the new one.

With no argument, cycles to the next break in link::Classes/Break#*order::.
argument:: name
a break key, an index, or nil to cycle.
returns:: this proxy
discussion::
code::
~d.brk(\amen); ~d.play;
~d.brk(\think);      // swap, gapless, on the bar
~d.brk;              // cycle
::

METHOD:: grind
The macro: how big the pieces are. Three exact stations — code::0:: one grain per
loop, code::0.5:: one grain per slice, code::1:: 64 grains per beat. Continuous
throughout, because a slice emphasis::is:: a big grain quantised to the grid.

note::
On its own this is nearly inaudible, and that is by design: pieces played in their
original order at their original times reassemble the original loop at any
resolution. code::grind:: does not transform the audio — it sets the
emphasis::resolution:: every other gesture then operates at. Pair it with
link::#-seq::, link::#-scr::, link::#-jit:: or link::#-over:: and the same scramble
is a beat shuffle at code::grind(0.3):: and a texture at code::grind(0.9)::.
::
argument:: x
0..1 (default 0.5)
returns:: this proxy

METHOD:: over
How far each grain overruns its own slot. Below 1 leaves gaps (an MPC gate feel), 1
abuts, above 1 overlaps and smears toward a pad. Gain-compensated, so the level
holds as you move it.
argument:: x
0.25..4 (default 1)
returns:: this proxy

METHOD:: scr
The probability, per slice, of ignoring the sequence and picking a slice at random —
a continuous fade from composed to stochastic, not a switch.
argument:: x
0..1
returns:: this proxy

METHOD:: jit
Grain position scatter: the ingredient that turns chops into dust.
argument:: x
0..1
returns:: this proxy

METHOD:: vari
How the loop is made to fit the tempo. code::1:: (default) repitches it — the
sampler/jungle sound, coherent at any code::grind::. code::0:: keeps the original
pitch and lets the grains stretch it, which needs grains: below about
code::grind(0.4):: each grain overruns its slice and drags the next slice's audio
in with it.
argument:: x
0..1 (default 1)
returns:: this proxy

METHOD:: speed
Musical transpose, on top of whatever code::vari:: is doing. Negative values play
backwards. Named code::speed:: because link::Classes/NodeProxy:: already has a
code::rate:: method (it reports audio vs control) and shadowing it would break
JITLib.
argument:: x
default 1
returns:: this proxy

METHOD:: pos
A static offset into the loop.
argument:: x
0..1
returns:: this proxy

METHOD:: flip
The probability that a grain plays backwards. A reversed grain starts at the far end
of the region it would have played forwards, so it covers the same audio.
argument:: x
0..1
returns:: this proxy

METHOD:: roll
Stutter: replay the first 1/n of each slice, n times. code::roll(1):: releases it.
argument:: n
1, 2, 4, 8… (default 2)
returns:: this proxy

METHOD:: win
The grain envelope. code::\punch:: (default) has a fast attack and short tail, so
chops keep their transient; code::\gate:: is near-rectangular and hardest;
code::\hann:: is the classic smooth granular window; code::\swell:: is reversed.

warning::
code::\hann:: wants code::over:: of 2 or more. Its edges are half the grain each way,
so at code::over(1):: consecutive grains do not overlap enough to sum back to a flat
level and the output is gated at the slice rate — measured 95 dB of dip per seam at
code::over(1)::, and flat to within 3 dB at code::over(2)::. The punchier windows are
the ones that behave at code::over(1)::.
::
argument:: name
returns:: this proxy

METHOD:: seq
The slice order, rewritten in place on the next bar — nothing is rebuilt. In a
string, digits then letters index slices (base 36), code::.:: and code::~:: are
rests, spaces and code::|:: are visual only. An array works too, with nil,
negative and link::Classes/Rest:: entries as rests.

A sequence may be longer than one loop; it keeps counting across loops instead of
restarting, which gives polymeter.
argument:: spec
a link::Classes/String:: or link::Classes/Array::
returns:: this proxy
discussion::
code::
~d.seq("0325");
~d.seq("0.32 0.15");                  // "." rests, spaces cosmetic
~d.seq("0123456789abcdef012345");     // 22 steps against a 16-slice loop
::

METHOD:: ord
Back to source order, and code::scr:: to 0.
returns:: this proxy

METHOD:: rev
The loop backwards by slice (not by sample), and code::scr:: to 0.
returns:: this proxy

METHOD:: mask
Euclidean gate: keep code::hits:: slices out of code::n::, rest the others. Uses the
Bjorklund quark.
argument:: hits
argument:: n
defaults to the current slice count.
returns:: this proxy

METHOD:: steps
How many slices the loop is cut into — 16 gives sixteenths of a 4-beat loop. If the
current order is untouched it is regenerated at the new length; a sequence you set
by hand is left alone.
argument:: n
1..64 (default 16)
returns:: this proxy

METHOD:: resync
Realign the loop, the slice grid and the sequence to the bar. Alignment is exact
when a voice starts and after any resync; a mid-set tempo change can leave the loop
boundary off the bar until you ask for this.
returns:: this proxy

subsection:: Synesthesia painting moves

The verbs of link::Classes/Pattern#-darker#the painting register::, on the proxy
that holds the sound. A proxy whose source is a pattern has that pattern moved
and reassigned (crossfaded, on the grid, like any reassignment). A proxy whose
source is a synth has its code::hue::/code::sat::/code::val:: controls moved from
their current values — so a drone recipe joins the register the moment it names
those controls — and a source that speaks none warns and does nothing.

METHOD:: darker
METHOD:: lighter
METHOD:: paler
METHOD:: saturate
METHOD:: warmer
METHOD:: cooler
See link::Classes/Pattern#-darker::. Each returns this proxy.
argument:: x
the step (default 0.2; 0.1 for warmer/cooler).
returns:: this proxy

METHOD:: paint
Recolour what the proxy holds: a pattern source samples the colour from now on, a
synth source has its axes set to the tube's centre.
argument:: color
a colour (any form link::Classes/Paint#*color:: accepts).
returns:: this proxy
discussion::
code::
~pad = \orange.brush("Cm7 G7");  ~pad.play;
~pad.darker(0.3);          // the pattern is reassigned darker
~pad.paint(\violet);       // and recoloured

~a = \lit.drone(~r);  ~a.play;    // a recipe whose graph reads \val.kr
~a.darker;                 // moves the control: ~a.set(\val, ...)
::
